from collections import deque

class Solution:
    def isCousins(self, root, x, y):
        q = deque([(root, None)])

        while q:
            parents = {}

            for _ in range(len(q)):
                node, parent = q.popleft()

                if node.val == x:
                    parents[x] = parent

                if node.val == y:
                    parents[y] = parent

                if node.left:
                    q.append((node.left, node))

                if node.right:
                    q.append((node.right, node))

            if x in parents or y in parents:
                return x in parents and y in parents and parents[x] != parents[y]

        return False
Complexity Analysis
Time Complexity
O(n)

Each node in the binary tree is visited at most once.

Space Complexity
O(n)

The queue can contain up to n nodes in the worst case.

Key Concept
Breadth-First Search (BFS)
Level Order Traversal

BFS is useful here because nodes at the same depth are processed together.

Result

The solution successfully determines whether the two given nodes are cousins in a binary tree using level-order traversal and parent tracking.
